course: geography
lesson: 34
title: Muscovy, England and France: how the big powers grew
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Big countries were not born big: in 1337 Moscow was a small principality paying tribute to the Golden Horde, England still held Gascony in France, and the French king ruled directly only part of what is France today.
explain: They grew like a snowball rolling downhill, picking up land by war, by marriage, by inheritance and by deals, and sometimes losing it again, as England lost almost all its French lands by 1453.
explain: Europa Universalis 5 lets you "form Great Britain" or push Muscovy across Siberia with a few clicks, but in real life each step took decades and cost the lives and freedom of ordinary people.
explain: Russian serfs were bound to the land and could be sold with it, Irish Catholics lost most of their land to settlers, and Siberian peoples lost their hunting grounds to fur traders.
explain: At the very end of the game's period the French Revolution and Napoleon turned the map of Europe upside down, and in 1815 the Congress of Vienna redrew it again.
fact: Moscow was a small principality, a land ruled by a prince, under the overlordship of the Golden Horde in 1337, and it grew into the centre of a large Russian state; Ivan III stopped paying tribute, the yearly payment of a ruled land to its overlord, to the Horde in 1480.
fact: Russia expanded east across Siberia from the 1580s and reached the Pacific coast by the 1640s, largely in search of furs.
fact: Siberian peoples such as the Khanty, Evenks and Yakuts lost land, and many died from new diseases and violence.
fact: In Russia most peasants became serfs, bound to their landlord's estate and not free to leave, until serfdom was abolished in 1861.
fact: Peter the Great built St Petersburg on the Baltic coast from 1703 and made it Russia's capital, giving Russia a sea port facing western Europe; many forced labourers died building it on marshy land.
fact: England began as a kingdom on part of one island, and in 1337, when EU5 starts, it still held lands in France, such as Gascony.
fact: The Hundred Years' War between England and France began in 1337, and by 1453 England held only Calais in France.
fact: England and Scotland joined to form the Kingdom of Great Britain in 1707, and Ireland joined in 1801 to make the United Kingdom.
fact: English and later British rulers took land from Irish Catholics and gave it to Protestant settlers, especially in the 1600s, so that by the 1700s Catholics owned only a small share of the land.
fact: France grew from the lands around Paris held by its kings into a large, united kingdom by absorbing great territories such as Burgundy and Brittany; Brittany joined through royal marriages and was formally united with France in 1532.
fact: Louis XIV ruled France from 1643 to 1715 and built the palace of Versailles, making France the strongest power in Europe, though his wars left France deep in debt.
fact: The French Revolution began in 1789, ended the monarchy in 1792 and spread new ideas about rights and citizens across Europe.
fact: Napoleon's empire at its height, around 1810 to 1812, controlled or dominated most of western and central Europe, and after his defeat the Congress of Vienna redrew the map in 1815.
exercise: Who was Moscow's overlord in 1337? | The Golden Horde | The Ottoman sultan | The King of France | The Pope in Rome and his bishops
exercise: When did Ivan III stop paying tribute to the Horde? | 1480 | 1337 | 1703 | 1861
exercise: When did Russia reach the Pacific coast? | By the 1640s | By the 1340s | Only in 1900 | In 1453, when Constantinople fell
exercise: What did Russians mainly want from Siberia? | Furs | Spices | Sugar | Olive oil
exercise: What happened to many Siberian peoples as Russia expanded? | They lost land and many died from disease and violence | They were given their own empire, ruled from a new capital | They all moved west to Moscow and became rich nobles at court | Nothing changed for them
exercise: What was a Russian serf? | A peasant bound to the landlord's estate | A soldier of the Golden Horde | A merchant who sailed the Caspian Sea to Persia | A priest in Moscow
exercise: When were Russian serfs freed? | 1861 | 1480 | 1703 | 1789
exercise: Why did Peter the Great build St Petersburg on the Baltic? | To give Russia a port and a door to western Europe | Because Moscow had burned to the ground the year before | Because the Pope ordered him to | To be closer to China and its silk
exercise: Which French land did England still hold in 1337? | Gascony | Burgundy | Brittany | Provence
exercise: What did England lose in the Hundred Years' War? | Almost all its lands in France except Calais | Scotland and Wales, which became one kingdom | Ireland and Cornwall | Its whole navy, sunk by the French at sea
exercise: When did England and Scotland become one kingdom? | 1707 | 1337 | 1453 | 1801
exercise: What happened in 1801? | Ireland joined to make the United Kingdom | Scotland joined England to make Great Britain | England finally lost Calais to France | Napoleon became emperor
exercise: How did English rule change who owned land in Ireland? | Land was taken from Catholics and given to Protestant settlers | Every Irish family got more land than before | Land was given to French nobles who had fled their revolution | Nothing changed at all
exercise: Name one way French kings gained new lands. | Royal marriages, as with Brittany | Buying land from the Ottoman sultans | Draining the Mediterranean Sea | Winning a football match
exercise: Who built the palace of Versailles? | Louis XIV | Napoleon | Peter the Great | Ivan III
exercise: What did the French Revolution end in 1792? | The French monarchy | The Hundred Years' War | Serfdom in Russia and Poland | The Republic of Venice
exercise: What happened to the map of Europe after Napoleon was defeated? | The Congress of Vienna redrew it in 1815 | It stayed exactly as Napoleon left it | France took all of Russia and Poland as a reward | Every border was erased
test: In EU5 you "form Great Britain" with one decision. How did it really happen? | England and Scotland joined in 1707, and Ireland in 1801 | England conquered Scotland in one day in 1337 | Scotland bought England in 1066 | It happened when Napoleon ordered it
test: My brother says Russia was always huge. Wrong? | Yes, in 1337 Moscow was a small principality under the Horde | No, Russia has always reached the Pacific, even in 1337 | No, Moscow ruled over the whole Golden Horde in 1337 and beyond | Yes, Russia was part of France then
test: If the fur trade had been worthless, what would most likely have been different? | Russia would have had less reason to push across Siberia so fast | Russia would have reached America in 1340 | Peter the Great would never have been born | Serfdom would have ended in 1480
test: my mate reckons Louis XIV made France super rich. not exactly, is it | Right, France was strong but his wars left it deep in debt | No, Louis XIV made France the poorest country in the whole of Europe | No, he never fought any wars | Right, Louis XIV lived in Russia
test: A Russian peasant in 1800 wants to move to the city for work. What stops him? | As a serf he is bound to the estate | Cities did not exist in Russia | He needs a passport for Schengen | Nothing stops him at all
test: Why did the English lose Gascony? | It was taken by France in the Hundred Years' War | They sold it to the King of Spain for a ship full of gold | The sea flooded it | It joined Scotland
test: Peter the Great's new capital was built on marshy land. Who paid the price? | Tens of thousands of forced labourers, many of whom died | Nobody, because paid engineers from Holland built it with machines | Only the Tsar's own family | Merchants from Venice
test: which year did the French Revolution kick off, quick | 1789 | 1337 | 1707 | 1815
test: A friend says France was always one united kingdom. What is wrong? | Kings slowly absorbed lands such as Burgundy and Brittany | Nothing, France never changed | France was ruled by Russia | France was only Paris until 1900
test: Around 1810, which ruler controlled or dominated most of western and central Europe? | Napoleon | Louis XIV | Peter the Great | Ivan III
test: A history card says Irish Catholics owned most land in Ireland by 1750. Is it right? | No, by then Catholics owned only a small share | Yes, settlers never came | Yes, the English gave them extra land as a reward for loyalty | No, the French owned it all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of the principality of Moscow in 1337? | A modest principality subject to the overlordship of the Golden Horde | The capital of an empire extending from the Baltic Sea to the Pacific Ocean | A province of the Ottoman Empire | A possession of the English Crown
exam: State the year in which serfdom was abolished within the Russian Empire. | 1861 | 1789 | 1707 | 1480
exam: Identify the instruments whereby the United Kingdom was constituted. | The union of England and Scotland in 1707 and of Ireland in 1801 | A single treaty with France in 1453 | The Congress of Vienna in 1815 | A decree of Louis XIV in 1643
exam: Ascertain which territory alone remained in English possession in France subsequent to 1453. | Calais | Gascony | Brittany | Burgundy
